{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate an unbounded space of implicit, cross-module invariants that are never documented because no single change introduces them; instead, they are silently welded together by sequences of locally-correct commits. Because every individual modification preserves the invariants it can see, testing and review validate only the explicit contract, leaving the shadow contract intact but increasingly fragile. The system becomes harder to change not because its visible complexity grows, but because the fraction of load-bearing constraints that exist outside the code's explicit representation asymptotically approaches totality."}
